{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1791447561,"nonce":"w6uEBAZ4JRecDzQXHGT4nU9H2tA=","message":"Implemented a candidate post-egraph CLIF scheduling pass in Zyntax, using the existing direct machine-code installation path. It runs Cranelift optimization once, then compiles the revised layout directly through TargetIsa::compile_function; dependency pin and tier policy are unchanged. Only scalar f32/f64 add/subtract/multiply/divide/FMA move, within their existing block, when every operand is available before an intervening call and at least two distinct operands have exactly one global use. Aliases and successor operands are included in use counts. Loads, traps, branches, calls and constants are not moved.\n\nThe first implementation inserted arithmetic immediately before the call. A compile-cost probe caught substantially worse register-allocation time with 30/300 simultaneous sums. The revised implementation places each operation directly after its last defining operand, also reducing pressure before the call. This removes that compile-cost regression on ARM64. The final placement is now undergoing NUC validation before fixed-binary LLVM-off timing. No performance result is accepted yet.\n\nARM64 final candidate: 428 compiler unit tests plus aggregate snapshot, load-region, 14 OSR-transfer and promoted-stub regressions pass. New tests assert six native FP spills become three for f32 and f64, preserve IEEE results across calls that overwrite both input arrays (including aliased output), decline shared/duplicate/unavailable inputs, and preserve dependency order across multiple calls. No code committed or issue closed yet.","files":null}]}